{
  "_comment": "Pricing calibration. The engine multiplies each component by the factor that applies to it, so a generated quote can be brought into line with the agency's published package prices without touching the rate card itself. Produced by `npm run calibrate -- --write`, and safe to hand-edit afterwards — your edits survive the next run unless you pass --write again.",
  "_howToUse": "Run `npm run calibrate` to see how every published package prices today. Where the generated price is wrong, either correct the underlying rate in Pricing_Rules.xlsx (best), or set a factor here (fastest). A factor of 1.0 is neutral; 1.15 makes that component 15% dearer.",
  "_precedence": "package > country > region > global. The first match wins, they do not compound.",
  "_countryFit": "Country and region entries below the Southeast Asia and Vietnam notes were fitted by `npm run calibrate -- --write` against the agency's own published package prices (3 Star couple, land only, 1.05x the brochure), bounded to 0.4-2.5 (widened from 1.5 once the agency confirmed several regions were that far under). India and Vietnam are excluded: the agency checked those line by line. Packages whose published price is under 0.25x or over 4x the bottom-up quote (Maharashtra at Rs 5,00,000) are ignored as data errors (Maharashtra was one: Rs 5,00,000 for 11 nights, corrected to Rs 50,000 in the dataset). Re-run the command after changing rates; it multiplies onto the factor already in force.",
  "global": {
    "hotel": 1,
    "flight": 1,
    "airport-transfer": 1,
    "local-transport": 1,
    "ground-transport": 1,
    "intercity-transport": 1,
    "sightseeing": 1,
    "meals": 1,
